### Why is choosing a shard key so important?

The decision is effectively permanent. Once data is distributed and the application routes by that key, changing it means re-sharding the entire dataset.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ShardKeyImportance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Shard key: the MOST important decision in sharding");
        System.out.println("  Once chosen, changing it = re-shard entire dataset");
        System.out.println("  Re-sharding = move terabytes of data while serving traffic");
        System.out.println("  One of the most painful operations in distributed databases");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Two properties to balance:");
        System.out.println("  1. Even distribution (no hot shard)");
        System.out.println("  2. Query locality (common queries hit one shard)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Shard key: the MOST important decision in sharding
  Once chosen, changing it = re-shard entire dataset
  Re-sharding = move terabytes of data while serving traffic
  One of the most painful operations in distributed databases

Two properties to balance:
  1. Even distribution (no hot shard)
  2. Query locality (common queries hit one shard)
```

### What is a hot shard and why does it cap scalability?

A sharded cluster is only as fast as its busiest shard. If one shard holds or serves far more than its share, it saturates while others sit idle.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class HotShard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hot shard scenario:");
        System.out.println("  4 shards, each should handle ~25% of traffic");
        System.out.println("  Bad key: shard-0 gets 70%, shards 1-3 get 10% each");
        System.out.println("  Shard-0 saturates at 5000 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  Total system capped at ~7000 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  Adding more shards does NOT help");
        System.out.println("  The hot shard IS the bottleneck");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Fix: re-shard with a better key (painful but necessary)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Hot shard scenario:
  4 shards, each should handle ~25% of traffic
  Bad key: shard-0 gets 70%, shards 1-3 get 10% each
  Shard-0 saturates at 5000 req/sec
  Total system capped at ~7000 req/sec
  Adding more shards does NOT help
  The hot shard IS the bottleneck
```

### What causes a hot shard?

Three common causes: low-cardinality keys, monotonic keys, and popularity-correlated keys.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class HotShardCauses {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Cause 1: Low cardinality");
        System.out.println("  Key: country (India/USA/UK)");
        System.out.println("  90% of customers are in India -> one shard gets 90%");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cause 2: Monotonic key");
        System.out.println("  Key: auto-increment ID with range sharding");
        System.out.println("  All new accounts go to the latest shard -> always hot");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cause 3: Popularity-correlated");
        System.out.println("  Key: merchant_id");
        System.out.println("  Amazon's merchant_id on one shard -> massive traffic");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Cause 1: Low cardinality
  Key: country (India/USA/UK)
  90% of customers are in India -> one shard gets 90%

Cause 2: Monotonic key
  Key: auto-increment ID with range sharding
  All new accounts go to the latest shard -> always hot

Cause 3: Popularity-correlated
  Key: merchant_id
  Amazon's merchant_id on one shard -> massive traffic
```

### Why must the key provide query locality?

A query without the shard key cannot be routed to one node. It scatter-gathers to every shard and merges.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class QueryLocality {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Good locality (shard key = customer_id):");
        System.out.println("  SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE customer_id = 'SAV2001'");
        System.out.println("  -> Router sends to ONE shard -> fast");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Bad locality (shard key = random hash):");
        System.out.println("  SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE customer_id = 'SAV2001'");
        System.out.println("  -> customer_id is NOT the shard key");
        System.out.println("  -> Router sends to ALL 4 shards -> scatter-gather -> slow");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Good locality (shard key = customer_id):
  SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE customer_id = 'SAV2001'
  -> Router sends to ONE shard -> fast

Bad locality (shard key = random hash):
  SELECT * FROM transactions WHERE customer_id = 'SAV2001'
  -> Router sends to ALL 4 shards -> scatter-gather -> slow
```

### Why is customer_id usually the best key for banking?

High cardinality for even spread, plus natural filter for per-customer queries.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class CustomerIdAsKey {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("customer_id as shard key:");
        System.out.println("  High cardinality: millions of unique customers");
        System.out.println("  -> Hashing spreads evenly, no hot shard");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Common queries filter on customer_id:");
        System.out.println("    'Show Rajesh's accounts' -> one shard");
        System.out.println("    'Show Rajesh's transactions' -> one shard");
        System.out.println("    'Show Rajesh's statements' -> one shard");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Co-located data: accounts + transactions + statements");
        System.out.println("  All on the same shard for one customer");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
customer_id as shard key:
  High cardinality: millions of unique customers
  -> Hashing spreads evenly, no hot shard

  Common queries filter on customer_id:
    'Show Rajesh's accounts' -> one shard
    'Show Rajesh's transactions' -> one shard
    'Show Rajesh's statements' -> one shard

  Co-located data: accounts + transactions + statements
```

### Why is the shard key also the transaction boundary?

Single-shard operations keep ACID. Cross-shard operations need distributed coordination (Saga).

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class TransactionBoundary {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Shard key = transaction boundary:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Rajesh's accounts all on Shard 2:");
        System.out.println("    Transfer between Rajesh's savings and current");
        System.out.println("    -> Single shard -> ACID guaranteed");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Transfer from Rajesh (Shard 2) to Priya (Shard 3):");
        System.out.println("    -> Cross-shard -> NO single-node ACID");
        System.out.println("    -> Need Saga with compensating transactions");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Shard key = transaction boundary:

  Rajesh's accounts all on Shard 2:
    Transfer between Rajesh's savings and current
    -> Single shard -> ACID guaranteed

  Transfer from Rajesh (Shard 2) to Priya (Shard 3):
    -> Cross-shard -> NO single-node ACID
    -> Need Saga with compensating transactions
```

### What goes wrong with bad keys? Real examples.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class BadKeyExamples {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bad key 1: account_opening_date (monotonic)");
        System.out.println("  All new accounts -> latest shard -> HOT");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Bad key 2: region (low cardinality)");
        System.out.println("  80% of customers in Maharashtra -> one shard gets 80%");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Good key: customer_id (high cardinality + common filter)");
        System.out.println("  Even spread under hashing");
        System.out.println("  Per-customer queries stay on one shard");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Bad key 1: account_opening_date (monotonic)
  All new accounts -> latest shard -> HOT

Bad key 2: region (low cardinality)
  80% of customers in Maharashtra -> one shard gets 80%

Good key: customer_id (high cardinality + common filter)
  Even spread under hashing
  Per-customer queries stay on one shard
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Hash monotonic keys, co-locate related entities, handle naturally hot entities specially, and monitor per-shard skew.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Choose key: high cardinality + common query filter
  [x] Hash monotonic keys to kill write hot spots
  [x] Co-locate: customer + accounts + transactions on same shard
  [x] Handle hot entities (e.g. corporate mega-account) specially
  [x] Monitor per-shard: data size, CPU, connections, traffic
  [x] Shard key = transaction boundary: plan cross-shard flows
  [x] Prefer consistent hashing for growth
  [x] The fix for a bad key is a painful re-shard
```
